Theoretical Foundations of Entanglement-Based Tamper-Evident Sensor Networks over Deployed Fibre: 

Finite-Size Security Proofs, Stochastic Polarisation Control, and an Open Hybrid-Key Architecture

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: Deepseek, Meta AI 


Abstract

This thesis establishes the mathematical foundations for entanglement-based verification of climate sensor data integrity over deployed optical fibre, without assuming trust in the fibre infrastructure or classical network control. The work is entirely theoretical, providing rigorous security proofs, stochastic control theorems, scheduling guarantees, finite-size key-rate calculations, and an implementation architecture. We address three central challenges: (i) how to certify that sensor data has not been tampered with using quantum entanglement; (ii) how to combine quantum key distribution (QKD) with post-quantum cryptography (PQC) in a provably secure hybrid key combiner; and (iii) how to maintain the physical layer (polarisation) and network layer (scheduling) under realistic environmental and traffic conditions.

The principal contributions are as follows. First, we prove finite-size security bounds for two tamper-evidence protocols: a device-dependent protocol that assumes trusted measurement devices, and a device-independent protocol that makes no assumptions about the devices. For the device-dependent case, we use the Entropy Accumulation Theorem (EAT) to derive a min-entropy bound valid against arbitrary coherent attacks, removing the previously assumed IID restriction. For the device-independent case, we employ the correct quantum information-theoretic bound on the conditional von Neumann entropy as a function of the CHSH violation, yielding a valid min-tradeoff function for the EAT. Both theorems provide explicit finite-size corrections.

Second, we construct a hybrid QKD+PQC key combiner using a dual-PRF construction. We prove not only that the combiner is secure when both components are secure, but also that it remains secure when one component is completely compromised (robust security). This resolves the unproven dual-PRF assumption of HKDF and provides a composable security guarantee.

Third, we analyse polarisation drift on aerial fibre using stochastic differential equations. For open-loop drift (Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process), we derive exponential tail bounds on the fidelity. For closed-loop delayed proportional feedback, we prove a sufficient condition for mean-square boundedness using a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional, and obtain a second-moment bound independent of delay.

Fourth, we address entanglement scheduling under heterogeneous traffic. We prove stability of weighted max-weight scheduling for general classes, and derive a strict-priority bound for emergency traffic, ensuring that high-priority climate alerts are not starved by routine monitoring.

Finally, we provide a finite-size key-rate framework with realistic worked examples, showing that the device-dependent protocol yields positive key rates for block sizes of order $10^6$, while the device-independent protocol requires block sizes of order $10^8$ or larger at 62 km due to detection efficiency limitations. We present a layered architecture with control and telemetry interfaces suitable for human implementation teams, including a low-cost migration path from tamper detection to full QKD.

The thesis is self-contained: all assumptions are stated explicitly, all proofs are given in full, and all parameters are sourced from published experimental data (NIST, Brookhaven, USTC, ORNL). The central claim is not that quantum communication survives disasters better than classical fibre, but that entanglement can certify trust in climate data.

