Appendix D — Detailed Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Device-Dependent)

D.1 Protocol as an EAT Channel

The device-dependent protocol $\Pi_{\mathrm{TE-DD}}$ consists of $n$ rounds. In each round, the source distributes an entangled pair; Alice (sensor) and Bob (verifier) measure in either the $Z$ or $X$ basis, chosen randomly. After all rounds, they announce bases, sift to obtain the raw key, estimate the QBER $Q_{\mathrm{obs}}$ from a random subset, and apply error correction and privacy amplification.

The EAT is applied to the rounds that contribute to the key (data rounds). The test rounds (used for parameter estimation) are a fraction $\gamma$ of the total, but for simplicity we assume all rounds are data rounds and the QBER is estimated on a subset; the EAT can be adapted.

D.2 Min-Tradeoff Function

For BB84 with trusted devices, the min-tradeoff function is

f(Q) = 1 - h_2(Q),

where $Q$ is the quantum bit error rate. This is a valid lower bound on the conditional von Neumann entropy generated per round given the QBER. The justification is the standard reduction of BB84 to an entanglement distillation protocol: the phase error rate equals the bit error rate after symmetrisation, and the entropy per round is at least $1 - h_2(Q)$.

D.3 Application of EAT

We model the protocol as an EAT channel where the test statistic is the QBER. The alphabet size for data rounds is 2 (bits). The min-tradeoff function $f(Q)$ has variance bounded by a constant. Applying the EAT (Theorem B.1) with $S_{\mathrm{obs}} = Q_{\mathrm{obs}}$, we obtain

H_{\min}^{\varepsilon_s}(A|EC) \geq n (1 - h_2(Q_{\mathrm{obs}})) - c_1 \sqrt{n} \log(1/\varepsilon_{EA}) - c_2 \log(1/\varepsilon_s) - c_3,

where $A$ is the sifted key, $E$ is the adversary's side information, and $C$ is the public transcript (bases announcements, error correction information). The constants $c_1, c_2, c_3$ depend on the protocol parameters (e.g., test fraction, alphabet size). Explicit values can be computed using the EAT framework.

This proves Theorem 3.1.

