Appendix I — Finite-Size Key-Rate Formulas and Worked Examples

I.1 General Formula

The finite-size secret key length after privacy amplification is

\ell = H_{\min}^{\varepsilon_s}(A|EC) - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon_{PA}) - \operatorname{leak}_{EC},

where $\varepsilon_s$ is the smoothing parameter, $\varepsilon_{PA}$ is the privacy amplification error, and $\operatorname{leak}_{EC}$ is the error-correction leakage (typically $n f_{EC} h_2(Q_{\mathrm{obs}})$ with $f_{EC} \geq 1$).

I.2 EAT Constants

The constants $c_1, c_2, c_3$ (device-dependent) and $c_4, c_5, c_6$ (device-independent) are computed using the EAT framework. They depend on:

· The alphabet size of the data rounds (2 for bits).
· The test-round probability $\gamma$ (if applicable).
· The variance of the min-tradeoff function.
· The smoothing and error parameters.

The open-source library eatformework by Mironowicz and Bourennane provides numerical values. For our protocols, typical values are $c_1 \approx 5$, $c_2 \approx 2$, $c_3 \approx 1$ for device-dependent with $\gamma = 0$ (all rounds are data, QBER estimated on subset). For device-independent with $\gamma = 10^{-3}$, $c_4 \approx 10$, $c_5 \approx 2$, $c_6 \approx 1$.

I.3 Device-Dependent Example

Take $n = 10^6$, $Q_{\mathrm{obs}} = 0.03$, $\varepsilon_s = \varepsilon_{EA} = 10^{-9}$, $\varepsilon_{PA} = 10^{-9}$. The binary entropy $h_2(0.03) \approx 0.194$. The min-entropy bound is

H_{\min}^{\varepsilon_s} \approx 10^6 (1 - 0.194) - 5 \sqrt{10^6} \log(10^9) - 2 \log(10^9) - 1.

We have $\log_2(10^9) \approx 29.9$, $\sqrt{10^6} = 10^3$. So correction terms: $5 \cdot 10^3 \cdot 29.9 \approx 1.5 \times 10^5$, plus $2 \cdot 29.9 \approx 60$, plus 1. The raw entropy is $8.06 \times 10^5$ bits. After subtracting correction, we have about $6.5 \times 10^5$ bits. Subtract $2\log_2(1/\varepsilon_{PA}) \approx 2 \cdot 29.9 \approx 60$ bits and error-correction leakage (say $1.1 n h_2(0.03) \approx 2.13 \times 10^5$ bits). The final key length is positive, around $4 \times 10^5$ bits.

I.4 Device-Independent Example

Take $n = 10^6$, $\gamma = 10^{-3}$, $S_{\mathrm{obs}} = 2.4$, $\varepsilon_s = \varepsilon_{EA} = 10^{-9}$. The function $g(2.4) \approx 0.194$ (same numerical value as before, coincidentally). The number of data rounds $n_d = 0.999 \times 10^6 \approx 10^6$. The min-entropy bound is

H_{\min}^{\varepsilon_s} \approx 10^6 \cdot 0.194 - 10 \sqrt{10^6} \log(10^9) - 2 \log(10^9) - 1.

The correction term is $10 \cdot 10^3 \cdot 29.9 \approx 3 \times 10^5$ bits. The raw entropy is $1.94 \times 10^5$ bits, which is smaller than the correction. Hence the bound may be negative, indicating no positive key. To achieve positive key, one needs larger $n$ (e.g., $n = 10^8$ gives correction $\approx 10 \cdot 10^4 \cdot 29.9 \approx 3 \times 10^6$ bits, while raw entropy $\approx 1.94 \times 10^7$ bits, so positive) or higher $S_{\mathrm{obs}}$.

